- op: discuss  # 4251
  at: '2026-08-27T01:45:13Z'  # 4251
  by: agent  # 4251
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4251
  clock: 6  # 4251
  nonce: '4251'  # 4251
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-08-27, verbatim: \"in\".\n\nAsked: should std::os::*::fs be in the catalogue, given that this item names\nstd::os::windows::fs in scope while the catalogue header excludes std::os::* as\n\"raw-fd extensions\"? Presented with the scale correction that the ruling reaches\nstd::os::unix::fs too -- symlink, chown, fchown, lchown, chroot, mkfifo and the\npositional read/write family, on the platform the corpus actually runs -- and\nwith the recommendation to admit only names that reach a syscall while keeping\nthe raw-fd extension traits out, since those were the exclusion original target.\n\nSO THE SCOPE IS SETTLED AND IS WIDER THAN THIS ITEM AS FILED:\n  IN  - names in std::os::unix::fs and std::os::windows::fs that reach a\n        syscall. Windows: symlink_file, symlink_dir (fs_write),\n        FileExt::seek_read (fs_read), FileExt::seek_write (fs_write). Unix:\n        symlink, chown, fchown, lchown, chroot, mkfifo, plus the FileExt\n        read_at / write_at family. Stability to be checked per name, not\n        assumed from this list.\n  OUT - the raw-fd extension traits (AsRawFd / FromRawFd / IntoRawFd and\n        friends): they hand over a descriptor number and reach no syscall.\n        This was the actual target of the header exclusion and it stands.\n  OUT - MetadataExt / PermissionsExt / FileTypeExt / FileTimesExt accessors\n        and OpenOptionsExt builder setters: they read an already-fetched stat\n        struct or set a flag. Most of os::unix::fs by count is this.\n  OUT - unstable: junction_point, seek_read_buf, and any other checked as\n        unstable during the audit.\n\nWHY THE CONFLICT WAS REAL AND NOT A TYPO: std::fs::soft_link, the\ncross-platform way to create a symlink, IS catalogued, so before this ruling the\ntool flagged one way of creating a symlink and ignored the other. And a Linux\nprogram calling chroot or chown through std::os::unix::fs read as doing nothing\nat all there -- a false all-clear, which is the error direction this project\ntreats as worst.\n\nThe catalogue header records the conflict as open; it will be updated to record\nthis ruling instead. WI-tukif ADR-0016 provenance to be checked for the same\nexclusion so one fact does not keep two homes."  # 4251
- op: discuss  # 9683
  at: '2026-08-27T01:47:27Z'  # 9683
  by: agent  # 9683
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9683
  clock: 7  # 9683
  nonce: '9683'  # 9683
  message: "CORRECTION, 2026-08-27. I stated in PR #565 (body and commit message) and in\nmy notes that fs_write was \"the only one of the NINE kinds\" that could carry the\nlock family. There are EIGHTEEN: browser_storage_read, browser_storage_write,\ncommand_launch, db_read, db_write, env_read, env_write, external_potential,\nfs_read, fs_write, host_info_read, ipc_recv, ipc_send, logging, net_recv,\nnet_send, process_send, subprocess.\n\nTHE DECISION IS UNCHANGED and I re-checked it against the full set rather than\nassuming: ipc_send is the only other plausible home for a file lock and it\nimplies a MESSAGE, which a lock is not; env_write is process-state mutation\n(chdir, umask, setuid); the rest are unrelated. fs_write remains the fit, for\nthe reason already recorded. But the stated reason was wrong, and a wrong count\nin a merged artifact is the kind of thing a later reader would rely on.\n\nWHERE THE ERROR CAME FROM: my own working notes carried \"nine boundary kinds\"\nand I did not check it against KNOWN_IO_BOUNDARIES before shipping. A NUMBER\nCARRIED IN A NOTE IS NOT A MEASUREMENT. Notes corrected."  # 9683
