- op: discuss  # fa5c
  at: '2026-08-27T02:37:20Z'  # fa5c
  by: agent  # fa5c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # fa5c
  clock: 10  # fa5c
  nonce: fa5c  # fa5c
  message: "ENUMERATION COMPLETE FOR ALL FIVE MODULES, 2026-08-27. PRs #565 and #567,\nmerged. 33 stable I/O primitives added in total. Marking done: this item is the\nENUMERATION, and module_completeness grants are the next arc step, not this one.\n\n  std::fs / std::fs::File / DirBuilder / DirEntry   #565, 20 rows\n  std::path / std::path::Path                       #565\n  std::os::unix::fs / std::os::windows::fs          #567, 13 rows\n  std::path::PathBuf                                NO ROWS -- see below\n\nPathBuf IS SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY REASONING, which is what the open\nquestion on this item asked for. The worry was that PathBuf Derefs to Path, so\n`pb.exists()` might be attributed to PathBuf and need duplicate rows. Measured\nagainst the 6 rust-bearing surveys, counting what the analyzer ACTUALLY stamps:\n\n    std::path::Path        20 edges   names: module(14), new(7), is_separator(1)\n    std::path::PathBuf      7 edges   names: module(5), from(2)\n\nNOT ONE I/O METHOD IS ATTRIBUTED TO EITHER. No exists, metadata, read_dir,\ncanonicalize -- only imports and associated-function calls. That is INV-linub's\nL3 finding confirmed at this level: rust stdlib method calls carry slot\n`external`, so `path.exists()` never lands on std::path::Path at all.\n\nSO ADDING PathBuf METHOD ROWS WOULD BE STRICTLY HARMFUL, and now that is\nmeasured rather than derived: PathBuf has no I/O of its own (every own method is\nlexical), the Deref-inherited Path methods are never attributed to it, and rows\nwould make it METHOD-ONLY -- at which point the 2 real `PathBuf::from` edges in\nthe corpus would newly STARVE their repos. Zero matching benefit, real\nregression. PathBuf gets a positive no-I/O finding, no rows.\n\nTWO REFUSALS DECIDED HERE, both flagged in the original filing:\n\n* std::fs::ReadDir::next -- OUT. It genuinely reaches readdir(3), so this is a\n  refusal that needs its reason. The only way to obtain a ReadDir is\n  fs::read_dir or Path::read_dir, BOTH CATALOGUED, so omitting `next` cannot\n  produce a false all-clear -- the I/O is already caught at the entry point.\n  Against that, `next` is the most generic name in the language; cataloguing it\n  would need a module hint that rust edges mostly lack (INV-linub), so it would\n  either match nothing or match everything.\n\n* File's Read/Write/Seek trait impls -- NOT THIS ITEM, and WI-gihos now has\n  concrete evidence to work from. Measured: std::io::Write carries 4 rows\n  (write, write_all, write_fmt, flush) while STD::IO::READ CARRIES ZERO, as do\n  std::io::Seek and std::io::BufReader. So the tool can see a file WRITTEN\n  through the trait and cannot see one READ through it. That asymmetry is\n  WI-gihos's scope, and it is a real gap, not a stylistic one.\n\nA THIRD PROBE BUG, same shape as the first two. Trait methods are declared\ninside `pub trait X {` as bare `fn name(&self..`, not `pub fn`, so a probe keyed\non `pub fn` reports every extension trait as EMPTY. Three for three this\ncampaign: every probe bug returned a plausible wrong answer, never an error.\nA PROBE THAT UNDER-REPORTS IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A MODULE WITH NO SURFACE.\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM DID NOT BUY, stated because the filing promised it: no verdict\nmoved. Measured both times across 42 surveys -- starved-module set UNCHANGED,\nnothing freed, nothing newly starved. That is the PAY EXPECTATION this item\nrecorded up front: the uncatalogued-module rung is the LAST of six, and\nstd::path::Path still starves in 3 of 3 rust repos on the rung above it. The\nenumeration is what makes the grants possible; the grants are the next step."  # fa5c
